<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shuwa Arabs</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shuwa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> are an Arabic-speaking people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> region, living in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>, and a branch of the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baggara-arabs">Baggara Arabs</a>. They descend from Arab groups who moved west across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> over several centuries from about the 14th century, and are mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> herders. In Borno they have long lived alongside the Kanuri, and their dialect is known as Shuwa or Chadian Arabic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shuwa-arabs.jpg" alt="Shuwa Arabs" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: YACOUB DOUNGOUS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caravane_des_nomades_10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
